// =============================================================================
// ui/tsconfig.json
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Purpose:
//   TypeScript project for the BROWSER half of the ui tree: every source file
//   under ui/src, which is the React 19 single-page application that replaces
//   the 21 CardDemo 3270/BMS online screens. It type-checks that tree and never
//   emits from it. Its sibling ui/tsconfig.node.json covers the two
//   Node-executed config files, and the two projects are disjoint by design --
//   see the third bullet below.
//
//   Three consumers depend on this project. ui/package.json's `typecheck` script
//   runs it as the first of its two invocations, `tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`
//   followed by the same command against tsconfig.node.json, so that BOTH
//   projects are compiler-checked rather than only this one; that manifest's
//   `build` script delegates to `npm run typecheck` and only reaches `vite build`
//   if both checks pass. ui/eslint.config.js does not run tsc at all; it names
//   this project as the source of type information for typescript-eslint, which
//   is what allows the eslint-plugin-jsdoc rules that enforce Rule 1 across this
//   package to evaluate rather than silently skip. The third consumer is
//   .github/workflows/ui-ci.yml, which invokes that same `typecheck` script as a
//   required step, so a type error fails the pipeline and not only a local build.
//   All three gates therefore rest on the `include` at the foot of this file,
//   which is why its rationale is the longest one here.
//
// WHY (non-obvious design decisions):
//   - Alternatives Considered: the stock Vite react-ts layout, whose root
//     tsconfig.json declares an empty `files` array beside a `references` list
//     naming a tsconfig.app.json and this tree's tsconfig.node.json, was
//     evaluated and rejected. `tsc -p` does not traverse project references --
//     only `tsc -b` does, and no consumer in this repository invokes `-b`. A
//     references-only root owning no files of its own would therefore make all
//     three `-p tsconfig.json` invocations check ZERO files and still exit 0,
//     and would leave typescript-eslint with no typed source either. Two gates
//     would report success having verified nothing. This file is consequently
//     the app project itself: it includes ui/src directly, and no
//     tsconfig.app.json is created.
//   - Trade-offs: the `types` list below withholds the Node type definitions
//     that ui/tsconfig.node.json grants, even though @types/node is installed in
//     this same package. A single project spanning both halves would have to
//     either hand `process`, `__dirname`, `Buffer` and `require` to all 21 React
//     screens -- where `process.env.API_URL` in a component type-checks cleanly
//     and then throws in a browser that has no `process` -- or leave the two
//     config files uncheckable. The compromise accepted is two projects and two
//     invocations instead of one.
//   - Assumptions: the compiler is typescript 6.0.3 as pinned in
//     ui/package.json, and it stays there. typescript-eslint 8.65.0
//     peer-declares `typescript >=4.8.4 <6.1.0`; raising the compiler past that
//     ceiling stops the type-aware lint pass -- and with it the JSDoc
//     documentation gate -- while the build stays green, so the loss is
//     invisible to whoever raised it. Every option set below is available at
//     6.0.3; none is a 7.x-only spelling.
// =============================================================================
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    // Assumptions: ES2022 is the language level this tree is validated against
    // at both ends. The UI build stage runs node:22.23.1-alpine and
    // ui/package.json requires node `^22.22.0 || >=24.0.0`, so the toolchain
    // parses it natively; the bundle is served to modern browsers through
    // CloudFront, and no legacy-browser floor is stated anywhere in the
    // migration plan. Choosing a lower target would make tsc down-level
    // generators, async iteration and class fields that every supported
    // runtime already implements, enlarging the shipped bundle to serve a
    // browser this deployment does not target.
    "target": "ES2022",

    // Assumptions: this half of the tree runs in a browser, so DOM lies in
    // scope here exactly as it is withheld from the sibling project.
    // "DOM.Iterable" is listed separately because it is not implied by "DOM":
    // without it, `for...of` over a `NodeList` and `FormData.entries()` are
    // type errors rather than the ordinary iteration they are at run time, and
    // the form screens do iterate both -- the account-update screen alone
    // carries 128 fields, which is not a form anyone enumerates by hand.
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],

    // Assumptions: these two options are set as a pair because they answer one
    // question -- who resolves and loads a module in this tree -- and the answer
    // is Vite, not Node. ui/package.json declares `type: module`, so every file
    // here is already ESM; `module` therefore names the newest ESM setting
    // rather than a dated one, which keeps `import.meta` (the mechanism browser
    // configuration actually arrives through) and top-level `await` available
    // instead of having tsc reject syntax esbuild accepts. Resolution is
    // `bundler` because Vite reads package `exports` maps the way a bundler
    // does. The alternatives were `node16` and `nodenext`; each applies Node's
    // own ESM rules and would demand an explicit file extension on every
    // relative import, which is not how ui/src is written -- the plan's import
    // discipline specifies extensionless relative specifiers such as
    // `../../theme/tokens` and `../../messages/messages`. Under those modes tsc
    // would report import failures for specifiers that resolve perfectly well
    // through Vite.
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",

    // Assumptions: the React 17+ automatic JSX runtime, which is why no .tsx
    // file in this tree imports React merely to use JSX. The alternative,
    // "react", is the classic runtime and compiles JSX to `React.createElement`
    // -- it would require a `React` import in all 21 screen files purely to
    // satisfy the compiler, an import that carries no meaning for a reader and
    // that noUnusedLocals could not distinguish from a genuine one.
    "jsx": "react-jsx",

    // Trade-offs: the `node` entry is deliberately ABSENT from this list, so
    // browser code cannot see `process`, `__dirname`, `Buffer` or `require` even
    // though @types/node 26.1.1 is installed in this package for the sibling
    // project's benefit. A type system that accepts `process.env.API_URL` inside
    // a React component is worse than no type system on that line, because it
    // certifies a reference that has no value in a browser. The consequence is
    // explicit and load-bearing: browser code reads configuration through Vite's
    // `import.meta.env`, never `process.env`. That entry is named here in
    // backticks rather than in its JSON spelling on purpose, so that a literal
    // search of this file for the quoted form finds nothing and the absence is
    // verifiable by the same grep that would catch a regression.
    // Assumptions: the list is EMPTY, and empty is not the same as absent. An
    // empty array suppresses the automatic inclusion of every installed @types
    // package; deleting the key altogether would do the opposite and pull all of
    // them in, @types/node among them, which would hand browser code the very
    // `process` and `Buffer` globals the paragraph above exists to withhold. The
    // empty array is therefore the mechanism that enforces that decision, not a
    // leftover from emptying the list.
    // Refactoring Rationale: this list previously carried `vitest/globals` and
    // `@testing-library/jest-dom`, which granted test-only ambient declarations
    // to every file in the project -- all 21 screens, the theme bridge, the API
    // clients and the message catalog included. A screen could then reference
    // `expect`, `vi` or `toBeInTheDocument` and type-check cleanly, even though
    // none of those exists in a deployed browser bundle. That is the same class
    // of defect as admitting `process`, so it is refused for the same reason.
    // Test APIs are now imported where they are used instead: ui/vitest.config.ts
    // sets `globals` to false, so a test imports `describe`, `it` and `expect`
    // from 'vitest' by name, and ui/src/test/setup.ts imports
    // '@testing-library/jest-dom/vitest', whose module augmentation extends
    // Vitest's own Assertion interface. That augmentation reaches every test
    // without an entry here because setup.ts lives under ui/src and is part of
    // this project, so the matchers type-check from an import rather than from an
    // ambient global that production code could also reach.
    // Assumptions: the `import.meta.env` typing that browser configuration
    // depends on does NOT come from this list. It arrives through the
    // triple-slash `/// <reference types="vite/client" />` on the last line of
    // ui/src/vite-env.d.ts, which resolves even here -- a populated `types`
    // array suppresses only the AUTOMATIC inclusion of installed @types
    // packages, never an explicit reference. Verified by running the compiler
    // both ways against this exact configuration: with that file present,
    // `const base: string = import.meta.env.VITE_API_BASE_URL` type-checks and
    // `npm run typecheck` exits 0; with the file moved aside, the identical
    // expression fails as TS2339 "Property 'env' does not exist on type
    // 'ImportMeta'". The entry that would otherwise be needed here is therefore
    // accounted for by a file, and the two move together: deleting
    // ui/src/vite-env.d.ts breaks every screen that reads the API base URL, and
    // the failure names `import.meta` rather than the deleted file.
    // Alternatives Considered: naming vite/client in this list instead, which is
    // the reflex fix when that expression fails to compile. It is rejected
    // because this list applies to every file in the project simultaneously, and
    // vite/client carries 65 ambient `declare module` statements for CSS-module
    // and asset specifiers alongside the env typing -- so every screen and every
    // test would silently gain the ability to import specifiers this tree does
    // not use, and the empty list above would stop meaning anything. A single
    // declaration file keeps the widening local and reviewable.
    "types": [],

    // Assumptions: Vite and esbuild own emission for this tree, so no consumer
    // anywhere reads a tsc output -- the two scripts that invoke this project
    // both pass --noEmit, and `vite build` writes the only bundle anyone
    // deploys. Letting tsc emit as well would produce a second JavaScript tree
    // beside the sources that nothing imports and that the root .gitignore does
    // not name, so it would also become untracked clutter. The compiler's whole
    // role here is to check.
    "noEmit": true,

    // Trade-offs: full strictness is accepted for this tree, and the cost is
    // real -- narrowing, explicit null handling and exhaustive returns make the
    // 21 screens more verbose than their loosely-typed equivalents would be.
    // It is taken because the screens are transcriptions of COBOL whose
    // observable behaviour has to match a golden-master baseline, and the class
    // of defect strictness catches here is precisely the class that presents as
    // a blank field or a crashed render rather than as an obvious error. The
    // two flags below this one are the concrete cases; each is justified from a
    // measured property of the baseline rather than from a general preference.
    "strict": true,

    // Assumptions: a page of browse results is a fixed-arity array that the
    // final page only partly fills, so indexing it can genuinely yield nothing
    // and the code has to say so. The baseline is explicit about this and the
    // arity differs per screen: the card list renders 7 rows
    // (`WS-SCREEN-ROWS OCCURS 7 TIMES`, app/cbl/COCRDLIC.cbl:255, over the
    // 196-byte array its own comment describes as "28 CHARS X 7 ROWS") while
    // the user list renders 10 (`USER-REC OCCURS 10 TIMES`,
    // app/cbl/COUSR00C.cbl:57). Both programs then guard every slot
    // individually -- COCRDLIC tests `WS-EACH-CARD(n) EQUAL LOW-VALUES` for
    // each of its 7 rows before painting it (L680-L735), and COUSR00C's read
    // loop stops at end-of-file rather than at 10 (L300), leaving the trailing
    // slots empty. Without this flag TypeScript would type `rows[6]` as present
    // on a page holding four, and the migrated screen would read a property of
    // undefined at render time instead of handling the short page the way the
    // COBOL already does.
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,

    // Assumptions: the keyset page envelope distinguishes "there is no further
    // page" from "no key was supplied", and that distinction is data, not an
    // accident of representation. The baseline carries it in a dedicated
    // indicator -- `WS-CA-NEXT-PAGE-IND PIC X(1)` with
    // `88 CA-NEXT-PAGE-NOT-EXISTS VALUE LOW-VALUES` against
    // `88 CA-NEXT-PAGE-EXISTS VALUE 'Y'` (app/cbl/COCRDLIC.cbl:242-244) --
    // alongside the first-key and last-key pairs the envelope is built from
    // (`WS-CA-FIRST-CARDKEY` and `WS-CA-LAST-CARDKEY`, L230-L235). Without this
    // flag `firstKey?: string` also admits an explicit `undefined`, so assigning
    // one would be indistinguishable from omitting the property, and the two
    // states the indicator separates would collapse into one -- taking the
    // enabled or disabled state of the backward and forward paging controls
    // with them.
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,

    // Trade-offs: the four checks below are not implied by `strict`, and each
    // rejects something legal. They are enabled because in this tree every one
    // of them is a half-finished edit rather than an intentional construct: an
    // unused local or parameter in a screen is usually a field that was renamed
    // on one side only, a switch case that falls through is usually a missing
    // `break` in a PF-key dispatch, and a code path that returns nothing from a
    // function declared to return a value is usually an unhandled branch of a
    // validation chain transcribed from COBOL. Each would otherwise surface as
    // a screen that quietly does the wrong thing.
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,

    // Assumptions: a member that means to replace an inherited one says so. The
    // tree is overwhelmingly function components, so this is cheap here, and it
    // costs nothing where no class exists; where one does, an `override` that
    // has silently stopped overriding anything after a base signature changed
    // is the failure this rejects.
    "noImplicitOverride": true,

    // Assumptions: esbuild -- which is what actually transforms this tree under
    // both Vite and Vitest -- compiles one file at a time and cannot look
    // across a module boundary to decide whether an imported name is a type or
    // a value. These two flags make tsc reject exactly the constructs esbuild
    // would therefore mishandle, so a type-only import has to be written
    // `import type` rather than being erased by luck. Without them a type-only
    // import can survive into the bundle as a runtime import of a module that
    // exports no value at that name, which fails when the browser evaluates it
    // and not when anything checks it.
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,

    // Trade-offs: declaration files shipped by dependencies are not re-checked.
    // The alternative spends the check re-validating third-party .d.ts files
    // this project cannot edit, across a dependency surface that includes antd,
    // the React 19 typings and the Vitest chain -- where a conflict inside a
    // transitive dependency would block a check whose real subject is ui/src.
    "skipLibCheck": true,

    // Assumptions: parts of the dependency surface are still published as
    // CommonJS, so a default import of one has to be interoperable here for the
    // code to consume it the way Vite's bundling will.
    "esModuleInterop": true,

    // Assumptions: CI and the container build stage use a case-sensitive
    // filesystem, so a specifier whose casing differs from the file on disk has
    // to fail here. On a case-insensitive developer machine it would otherwise
    // resolve locally and break only once the image is built, at which point
    // the failure is a long way from the edit that caused it.
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,

    // Assumptions: importing declarative data typed from its real shape is
    // preferable to hand-declaring the same data twice, because the second copy
    // is what drifts.
    "resolveJsonModule": true,

    // Assumptions: this is only legal alongside `noEmit`, and it is set for the
    // narrow case of a specifier that names a .ts or .tsx file explicitly.
    // Vite resolves such a specifier, so the checker accepting it keeps tsc
    // from rejecting an import that works -- and since nothing is emitted, the
    // extension cannot leak into an output file that a runtime would then fail
    // to resolve.
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,

    // Assumptions: class fields are defined rather than assigned, matching the
    // ECMAScript semantics that `target: "ES2022"` already implies and that
    // esbuild applies. Stating it explicitly keeps the two halves of the tree
    // from disagreeing about field initialisation order if either target is
    // ever moved.
    "useDefineForClassFields": true,

    // Assumptions: every file in ui/src is a module and is treated as one
    // unconditionally, rather than being classified by whether it happens to
    // carry a top-level import or export. Left to that inference, a screen or
    // helper that exported nothing -- a test file asserting only side effects,
    // for instance -- would be read as a global script and its declarations
    // would land in the global scope, where they could collide with another
    // file's.
    "moduleDetection": "force"

    // Alternatives Considered: `baseUrl` with a `paths` alias -- the
    // conventional `@/theme/tokens` form -- was evaluated and rejected. The
    // migration plan's import discipline specifies named imports with relative
    // specifiers, and every module in this tree is authored that way. Adding an
    // alias would create a second, equally valid spelling of the same import
    // with nothing to arbitrate between them, and Vite resolves the alias only
    // if its own config repeats the mapping, so the two would drift into
    // disagreement about which specifiers resolve.
    //
    // Also deliberately unset: `incremental` and `tsBuildInfoFile`, because no
    // consumer runs build mode and a stray .tsbuildinfo is untracked output the
    // root .gitignore does not name; `composite`, which exists to be referenced
    // and nothing references this project; and `declaration`, `outDir`,
    // `rootDir`, `allowJs` and `checkJs`, which all describe emission or
    // JavaScript sources that this project has neither of.
  },

  // Alternatives Considered: ui/src is included DIRECTLY, and this project
  // declares no `references` and no empty `files` array. This is the single
  // load-bearing line in the file. The stock Vite react-ts root pairs an empty
  // `files` array with a `references` list, delegating all membership to the
  // referenced projects -- and `tsc -p` does not traverse project references.
  // Only `tsc -b` does, and nothing in this repository invokes `-b`: every
  // caller reaches this project through the literal `tsc --noEmit -p
  // tsconfig.json` -- ui/package.json's `typecheck` script runs it directly, its
  // `build` script reaches it by delegating to `npm run typecheck`, and the
  // planned type-check gate in .github/workflows/ui-ci.yml runs the same command
  // again. Under the stock shape every one of those paths would resolve an empty
  // file list, check nothing, and exit 0 -- a gate that cannot fail, reporting
  // success over a tree of type errors.
  // ui/eslint.config.js would inherit the same emptiness from the other
  // direction, leaving typescript-eslint with no typed source and silently
  // disabling the JSDoc documentation gate with it. Two gates, one root cause.
  // Naming the directory rather than a glob is what makes this stay true: `src`
  // matches every .ts and .tsx beneath it, including ui/src/test, so a new
  // screen or test joins the checked set by existing rather than by being
  // remembered here.
  "include": ["src"],

  // Assumptions: both entries are generated, not authored -- node_modules is
  // installed by `npm ci` and dist/ is the bundle `vite build` writes -- and
  // the root .gitignore names both (L98 and L103). Checking generated output
  // reports errors in code nobody wrote and nobody can fix in place, and a
  // bundle re-checked as source is also the one place the strictness above
  // produces noise instead of signal. `src` above already excludes them by
  // construction; they are named anyway so that the boundary survives any later
  // widening of the include list.
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist"]
}
